/* Evidence levels for the Theism Probability Engine.
   s = Simple, i = Intermediate, e = Expert.  plain = one-line restatement shown at Simple level.
   ev = supporting points, ob = objections with a reply, who = key voices (Expert only). */
window.LEVELS={
/* ===== KALAM ===== */
k_p1:{plain:"Things do not just pop into existence for no reason. If something starts to exist, something made it start.",
 s:{ev:["Think of everything you know: a house, a baby, a song. Each one started because something caused it. We have never seen anything appear from literally nothing, so it is reasonable to think the rule holds for everything that begins."],
    ob:[{t:"Some tiny particles seem to appear with no cause.",r:"Those happen inside a physical world that already exists, with rules and energy. Nobody has seen something appear from truly nothing."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>The pattern never breaks.</strong> Everything we have traced back to its start had a cause. If that rule failed, it would be a mystery why horses, planets or universes do not pop up randomly all the time.","<strong>Quantum physics is not a counterexample.</strong> Quantum events happen in a structured vacuum with fields and laws, which is not 'nothing'. Critics of Lawrence Krauss's <em>A Universe from Nothing</em> made exactly this point."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Quantum events may be uncaused.</strong> Radioactive decay has no fixed trigger.",r:"Random is not the same as uncaused: the atom's state and the laws set the odds. The premise only needs <em>some</em> cause or condition, not a predictable one."},{t:"<strong>Our experience only covers things inside the universe.</strong> The rule might not stretch to the universe itself.",r:"That is the honest weak spot. Defenders say the rule rests on what it means to <em>begin</em>, not on where it happens. Critics call it a leap."}]},
 e:{ev:["<strong>Metaphysical, not merely inductive.</strong> Craig (<em>The Kal&amacr;m Cosmological Argument</em>, 1979; Craig &amp; Sinclair, 2009) defends the causal principle as a truth about being: nothing about 'nothing' could make one thing arise rather than another. Pruss defends the stronger Principle of Sufficient Reason, and Aristotelian and Thomist traditions treat causality as a first principle.","<strong>Hume's challenge.</strong> Hume (<em>Treatise</em> 1.3.3) argued we can conceive an uncaused beginning, so causation is not a priori. Anscombe (1974) replied that being able to picture an event without picturing its cause does not show it could lack one."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Quantum and cosmological exceptions.</strong> Quantum mechanics and no-boundary models such as Hartle&ndash;Hawking are offered as beginnings without prior causes. Oppy (<em>Arguing about Gods</em>) and Mackie (<em>The Miracle of Theism</em>) add that the principle may hold for things within time without covering the origin of time itself.",r:"Defenders reply that these models describe states within physics, not absolute nothing (see David Albert's review of Krauss), and that restricting the principle to 'inside time' is ad hoc. The dispute turns on whether causality is a feature of being or only of our experience."}],
    who:"Defend: Craig, Pruss, Koons. Dispute: Oppy, Mackie, Gr&uuml;nbaum."}},

k_p2:{plain:"The universe has not been around forever. It had a start, the Big Bang.",
 s:{ev:["Scientists measured that space is stretching. Run the film backwards and everything squashes into a very hot, tiny starting point about 13.8 billion years ago. That looks like a beginning."],
    ob:[{t:"The Big Bang might not be the very beginning, just a change from something earlier.",r:"Some scientists think that. But the best-tested pictures still point to a start, and the rest is argued about."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Big Bang cosmology.</strong> The expansion of space, the cosmic microwave background and the amounts of light elements all fit a hot, dense early state about 13.8 billion years ago.","<strong>A theorem points the same way.</strong> The Borde&ndash;Guth&ndash;Vilenkin theorem says that a universe that has been expanding on average cannot be past-eternal, and it covers many multiverse models too.","<strong>A philosophical argument.</strong> Defenders also argue that a beginningless past would mean an actually infinite number of past events, which they think is impossible."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>The Big Bang is where our physics stops working, not necessarily a start.</strong>",r:"Agreed that the equations break down. Defenders point to the BGV theorem, which does not need a singularity, and say the burden is on eternal-universe models."},{t:"<strong>Infinity is not impossible.</strong> Mathematicians use infinite sets all the time.",r:"Defenders say infinite sets are fine as ideas but cannot be real collections of past events. Critics say that claim has not been proved."}]},
 e:{ev:["<strong>Physics.</strong> Borde, Guth and Vilenkin (2003) show that any spacetime with a positive average expansion rate is geodesically incomplete to the past. Vilenkin has said the theorem leaves little room for a past-eternal expanding universe, while noting it does not say what came before the boundary. Craig and Sinclair add thermodynamic arguments and the failure of known eternal models (steady state, oscillating universes).","<strong>Philosophy.</strong> Craig's two arguments: an actual infinite cannot exist (Hilbert's Hotel), and a series formed by successive addition cannot be actually infinite, so the past is finite. Pruss defends a related 'causal finitism'."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Physics leaves it open.</strong> BGV is silent about what lies before the boundary. Aguirre and Gratton, and Carroll's models, give pictures with no beginning, and Hartle&ndash;Hawking gives a finite past without a first moment.",r:"Defenders say these models still need a boundary or a reversal of time's arrow, and that a finite past still means the universe 'begins' in the relevant sense. Critics reply that a boundaryless finite past is not obviously something that begins or needs a cause."},{t:"<strong>The philosophical arguments are contested.</strong> Morriston, Oppy and others argue Hilbert's Hotel shows only that infinite collections behave oddly, and that the successive-addition argument confuses potential and actual infinites.",r:"Craig, Sinclair and Pruss reply that the oddities become absurd when applied to real physical things. This is where many philosophers remain unconvinced."}],
    who:"Defend: Craig, Sinclair, Pruss. Dispute: Oppy, Morriston, Carroll, Aguirre."}},

k_pp1:{plain:"If the cause made space and time, it cannot itself be inside space and time.",
 s:{ev:["If the cause made all of space and time, it cannot be made of space or exist inside time, because those did not exist yet. So it has to be outside them."],
    ob:[{t:"Maybe it was something natural we have not discovered yet.",r:"Even then it would be outside our space and time, so beyond nature as we know it."}]},
 i:{ev:["The cause of space-time cannot itself be in space-time. So it is spaceless, timeless (at least before the first moment) and immaterial, since matter is part of the physical universe."],
    ob:[{t:"A timeless cause is hard to picture, since causes seem to come before their effects.",r:"Defenders say the cause could be timeless without the universe and in time with it, causing the first moment 'simultaneously'. Critics think this stretches the word 'cause'."}]},
 e:{ev:["Craig argues the cause is spaceless, immaterial and timeless 'sans creation' (changeless without the universe, temporal since it exists). It cannot be an abstract object because abstract objects have no causal power."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Timeless causation.</strong> Oppy and Mackie question whether a timeless cause can 'bring about' an effect or pick out a moment to do so.",r:"Craig appeals to simultaneous causation and a first free act. Critics doubt this makes sense."}],
    who:"Defend: Craig. Dispute: Oppy, Mackie."}},

k_pp2:{plain:"Making a whole universe from nothing takes a huge amount of power.",
 s:{ev:["Creating all of space, time and matter from nothing would take enormous power, far more than anything inside the universe has."],
    ob:[{t:"The cause might be strong, but not unlimited.",r:"Fair. The argument gets you to 'enormously powerful', not necessarily 'all-powerful'."}]},
 i:{ev:["The cause produced all physical reality with no material to work with, so its power has no obvious limit set by anything in the universe."],
    ob:[{t:"Enormous is not the same as all-powerful.",r:"Agreed. This premise only claims 'enough to create everything'. Other arguments are needed for more."}]},
 e:{ev:["A cause that brings the entire physical universe into being without pre-existing material or resistance must be vastly powerful. Whether it must be omnipotent is a separate step, usually argued from maximal-greatness considerations or a cumulative case (Swinburne)."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Underdetermination.</strong> Oppy and Mackie stress that cosmological arguments, even if sound, fix very few attributes: a being sufficient for one universe is not thereby the God of classical theism.",r:"Defenders agree it is only a partial case and combine it with other arguments in a cumulative case."}],
    who:"Defend: Craig, Swinburne. Dispute: Oppy, Mackie."}},

k_pp3:{plain:"If the cause was always there, why did the universe only start then? A mind can choose when to begin something.",
 s:{ev:["If the cause was always there, why did the universe start at one moment and not earlier? A thing that just follows rules would have caused it forever. A mind with a will can choose when to begin something."],
    ob:[{t:"Maybe the cause was a natural event, not a mind.",r:"Then it is hard to explain why a changeless natural cause produced a first effect at all."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>The puzzle of the first moment.</strong> If a sufficient cause never changes, its effect should be as old as it is. A free agent can bring about a first effect without any earlier change, which is why defenders say the cause is a mind.","<strong>Abstract things cannot cause anything.</strong> The only things we know of that start new chains of events from nothing earlier are agents."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Deciding looks like something that takes time.</strong>",r:"Defenders say the cause is timeless without the universe and acts freely at the first moment. Critics find this hard to make sense of."}]},
 e:{ev:["Craig argues that a permanent sufficient cause with a temporally first effect requires libertarian agent causation, otherwise the effect would exist as long as the cause does. Swinburne similarly argues that personal explanation is the simplest ultimate kind (<em>The Existence of God</em>)."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>A dilemma for the agent.</strong> Mackie and Oppy argue an agent needs a reason to act at a particular time. If the reason is eternal, nothing explains the beginning. Others suggest indeterministic non-personal mechanisms could also give a first effect.",r:"Defenders reply that a libertarian free agent needs no external sufficient reason, but that commits them to controversial views about freedom and timeless action."}],
    who:"Defend: Craig, Swinburne. Dispute: Mackie, Oppy."}},

k_pp4:{plain:"The chain of causes cannot go back forever, so something at the start was not caused.",
 s:{ev:["If every cause needed an earlier cause forever, nothing would ever have got started. So the chain has to stop at something that did not need a cause."],
    ob:[{t:"Why can't the chain just go on forever?",r:"If it did, there would be no first cause. Some philosophers accept that and others find it very hard to believe."}]},
 i:{ev:["A cause of everything that begins cannot itself have begun, or it would need a cause too. So it is uncaused. This 'first cause' idea runs from Aquinas and al-Ghazali to the kalam tradition."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Then why not say the universe is the uncaused thing?</strong>",r:"That is why premise 2 matters: the argument says the universe is the thing that began."}]},
 e:{ev:["By P1 whatever begins has a cause. If a beginningless chain of past causes is impossible (Craig, Sinclair; al-Ghazali; Bonaventure), the series must terminate in something that did not begin."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>The stopping point.</strong> Oppy argues that even with a finite past, the stopping point could be a brute physical state. 'Uncaused' is also ambiguous between 'necessary' and 'brute'.",r:"Defenders argue the stopping point must be non-contingent to avoid brute facts, which links this argument to the contingency argument."}],
    who:"Defend: Craig, Sinclair. Dispute: Oppy."}},

/* ===== FINE-TUNING ===== */
f_p1:{plain:"There are three possible reasons the universe suits life: it had to be that way, it was luck, or it was planned.",
 s:{ev:["If something is just right for life, there are only three ways to explain it: it had no choice, it was luck, or it was planned. This step only says those are the options."],
    ob:[{t:"Maybe there is a fourth option we have not thought of.",r:"Possibly. Some say 'we could only exist in a universe like this', but defenders say that just describes the luck without explaining it."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>The menu.</strong> Necessity (the constants could not have been otherwise), chance (a fluke, or one of many) and design. The argument only needs these to be the live options.","<strong>The multiverse</strong> is usually filed under 'chance', since it makes our universe one of many."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>We might be missing an option.</strong>",r:"That is true of any 'best explanation' argument. Defenders say we should compare the rivals we can actually name."}]},
 e:{ev:["Collins (<em>Blackwell Companion to Natural Theology</em>, 2009) frames the choice as necessity, chance or design, and the argument as a likelihood comparison: how probable the life-permitting data are on theism versus on naturalism with a single universe, with the multiverse treated as a separate rival."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Selection effects and 'necessity'.</strong> Sober argues that observing a life-permitting universe is guaranteed by our existence, so the evidence must be conditioned carefully. Some physicists hope for a deeper theory that fixes the constants (necessity).",r:"Collins and Barnes reply that selection effects explain why we observe such a universe, not why one exists at all."}],
    who:"Defend: Collins, Swinburne. Dispute: Sober, Stenger."}},

f_p2:{plain:"It is very unlikely to be luck, and nothing says it had to be this way.",
 s:{ev:["The settings that let stars, planets and life exist look balanced on a knife's edge. Change them a little and you get no atoms or no stars. Nothing in physics says they had to be this way, and luck seems very unlikely."],
    ob:[{t:"Maybe there are loads of universes and we are in the one that worked.",r:"That is the multiverse idea. It could explain the luck, but nobody can see other universes, and the thing that makes them might need fine-tuning too."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Narrow ranges.</strong> Physicists such as Martin Rees and Luke Barnes describe how small changes in numbers like the cosmological constant, the strong force or gravity would rule out stars, chemistry or long-lived universes.","<strong>Necessity looks unlikely.</strong> The values do not follow from any known deeper law.","<strong>Chance looks unlikely.</strong> The life-permitting range is tiny compared with the range that could have been."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>The multiverse.</strong> With huge numbers of universes, one like ours is no surprise.",r:"Defenders say a universe generator needs its own fine-tuning and there is no direct evidence for it. Critics say that standard is unfair, since the idea comes out of real physics."},{t:"<strong>We only have one universe to study.</strong> How can we say what is 'likely'?",r:"This is the main philosophical worry. Some say the probabilities are meaningless. Defenders say we can still compare ranges."}]},
 e:{ev:["<strong>Physics.</strong> The usual cases are the cosmological constant (Weinberg's 1987 anthropic bound), the fundamental force strengths, the carbon resonances and the low initial entropy (Penrose). Barnes (<em>PASA</em>, 2012) gives a careful survey and warns that not every claim is equally secure.","<strong>Epistemic probability.</strong> Collins uses a restricted principle of indifference over an 'epistemically illuminated' range of values, so that the life-permitting region is a tiny fraction. The data are very improbable on chance but not improbable on theism, since Swinburne argues God has reason to bring about conscious life."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>The measure problem.</strong> McGrew, McGrew &amp; Vestrup (<em>Mind</em>, 2001) argue that a uniform probability over an unbounded range of constants is not defined, so 'tiny fraction' claims fail. Colyvan, Garfield &amp; Priest (2005) press similar objections.",r:"Collins uses a bounded range and likelihood comparisons that avoid a full measure. Critics find the bounding arbitrary."},{t:"<strong>Multiverse and selection effects.</strong> Inflation and the string landscape (Susskind) make life-permitting universes expected somewhere, and Sober argues observation selection undermines the inference.",r:"Defenders cite the inverse gambler's fallacy (Hacking; White) against a multiverse explaining <em>this</em> universe, and argue the generator needs tuning. Critics deny the fallacy applies."}],
    who:"Defend: Collins, Swinburne. Careful survey: Barnes. Dispute: Sober, Colyvan, McGrew, Stenger; multiverse: Susskind."}},

f_pp1:{plain:"A designer of the whole universe cannot be a part of the universe.",
 s:{ev:["If the designer set up the whole universe, it cannot be one of the things inside it. It must be outside."],
    ob:[{t:"A designer might be inside a bigger universe that contains ours.",r:"Then you would need to explain that bigger universe too."}]},
 i:{ev:["The constants are features of the universe itself, so their setter must act from outside space-time, or the question just moves back a step."],
    ob:[{t:"Our universe might be a simulation designed by beings in another one.",r:"That is possible, but it pushes the question of who designed <em>that</em> universe."}]},
 e:{ev:["If the fundamental constants are brute features of this universe, their source is not a physical agent within it. Swinburne treats a single non-embodied intelligence as the simplest hypothesis; the simulation hypothesis is the main rival."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Finite designers.</strong> Hume's <em>Dialogues</em> (Philo) already noted that design arguments could support many, finite or flawed designers, and Oppy notes that a simulation-style designer would satisfy the data.",r:"Defenders reply that the cumulative case and simplicity favour a single transcendent designer, and that simulation only regresses the question."}],
    who:"Defend: Swinburne, Collins. Dispute: Hume (Philo), Oppy."}},

f_pp2:{plain:"Setting up a universe this precisely takes enormous intelligence.",
 s:{ev:["Getting so many settings right at once would take a mind far smarter than any human."],
    ob:[{t:"A smart designer is not necessarily a perfect one.",r:"True. The premise says 'supremely intelligent', not 'perfect'."}]},
 i:{ev:["The designer would need to understand the laws and mathematics well enough to arrange them so life is possible, which suggests an intellect far beyond ours."],
    ob:[{t:"The design may not be that clever: much of the universe is hostile to life.",r:"Defenders say fine-tuning is about making life <em>possible</em>, not making every place comfortable."}]},
 e:{ev:["The inference is to the intelligence needed to select life-permitting values among the space of possibilities; Swinburne argues that a bodiless, omniscient mind is the simplest limiting case of such intelligence."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Bad design.</strong> Hume and later critics (Draper) argue that cosmic wastefulness and suffering are better predicted by indifference than by a supreme intellect.",r:"Defenders reply that the argument supports intelligence, and that the problem of evil is a separate case to be weighed."}],
    who:"Defend: Swinburne. Dispute: Hume, Draper."}},

f_pp3:{plain:"Whoever set the universe up must be extremely powerful.",
 s:{ev:["Setting up the rules of the whole universe is a huge job, so the designer must be extremely powerful."],
    ob:[{t:"Powerful is not the same as all-powerful.",r:"Agreed. This step only claims 'enormously powerful'."}]},
 i:{ev:["Fixing the constants of the whole cosmos involves power on a scale beyond anything in the universe."],
    ob:[{t:"We only know the power was enough for this universe.",r:"True. This links to other arguments to get further."}]},
 e:{ev:["Selecting the values of the fundamental constants and laws of an entire cosmos plausibly requires power not limited by physical forces within it."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Underdetermination again.</strong> The data fix only that the designer was able to set these constants, as Oppy stresses.",r:"Defenders agree and appeal to cumulative case reasoning."}],
    who:"Dispute: Oppy."}},

f_pp4:{plain:"Someone who sets up a universe for life is thinking about life, so it must be a mind with a purpose.",
 s:{ev:["Fine-tuning is 'aimed' at making life possible. Having an aim means having a mind that wants something."],
    ob:[{t:"The universe might look aimed at life just by accident.",r:"That goes back to the luck options in the earlier steps."}]},
 i:{ev:["An unthinking force does not have goals. A designer who intends the existence of life is a person-like being with purposes."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Looks like aiming is not aiming.</strong> We see patterns even where there are no goals.",r:"Defenders say the argument is not just seeing patterns but a likelihood claim: the data are much more expected if someone intended life."}]},
 e:{ev:["On the likelihood framing, theism predicts life-permitting laws because a personal God has reasons to create conscious beings, while an impersonal mechanism has no such tendency (Swinburne; Collins)."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Probability of God's motives.</strong> Critics (Sober; Oppy) argue we have no independent way to know what a God would want, so the likelihood is unfixed.",r:"Defenders reply that moral reasons for creating persons are widely shared across theism, though this is not independent evidence."}],
    who:"Defend: Swinburne, Collins. Dispute: Sober, Oppy."}},

/* ===== MORAL ===== */
m_p1:{plain:"Without God, right and wrong would just be opinions.",
 s:{ev:["If there were no God, right and wrong would only be what people or evolution happen to feel. Nothing would make 'torturing for fun is wrong' true for everybody, whatever they think."],
    ob:[{t:"People can tell right from wrong without believing in God.",r:"Yes. The premise is about what makes morals real in the first place, not about whether atheists can be good."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>The grounding question.</strong> It is about what <em>makes</em> morals true, not whether atheists can be good. If morals are objective, something has to explain their authority and goodness.","<strong>Other explanations look shaky.</strong> Evolution, social agreement or feelings seem to describe what we do believe, not what we are bound to do."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Morals could be real without God</strong>, the way maths is.",r:"Defenders ask why abstract facts would give anyone <em>duties</em>. Critics say moral facts could just be basic."},{t:"<strong>Euthyphro.</strong> Is something good because God says so, or does God say so because it is good?",r:"Defenders say God's own good nature is the standard, so neither option applies."}]},
 e:{ev:["Craig argues that moral facts need a ground: goodness is rooted in God's nature as the paradigm of goodness, and duties in divine commands, which avoids the Euthyphro dilemma by locating the standard in God's character. Adams (<em>Finite and Infinite Goods</em>, 1999) defends a related theistic Platonism. Evans and Linville argue in different ways that naturalism lacks the resources for robust moral realism."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Secular robust realism.</strong> Parfit (<em>On What Matters</em>, 2011), Enoch (<em>Taking Morality Seriously</em>, 2011), Shafer-Landau and Wielenberg (<em>Robust Ethics</em>, 2014) defend irreducible moral facts needing no divine ground. Wielenberg adds that divine-nature accounts face their own grounding problem.",r:"Defenders say such views carry a mysterious ontology and cannot explain why moral facts bind us or match our beliefs. Critics reply that the same challenges apply to theism."}],
    who:"Defend: Craig, Adams, Evans. Dispute: Parfit, Enoch, Shafer-Landau, Wielenberg."}},

m_p2:{plain:"Some things are really wrong, like torturing a child for fun, whatever anyone thinks.",
 s:{ev:["Some things seem truly wrong everywhere and always, like torturing a child for fun. Most people are more sure of that than of any clever argument against it."],
    ob:[{t:"Feeling that something is wrong does not prove it is.",r:"True. But we trust our senses and reasoning the same way, so we need a good reason to distrust our moral sense."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Moral experience.</strong> We experience some acts as truly wrong, not just disliked.","<strong>Hard to deny in practice.</strong> Even people who say morals are opinions protest injustice as if it were really wrong.","<strong>Moral progress.</strong> Talk of reform and progress (abolition, human rights) assumes a real standard that societies can get closer to."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Evolution explains our moral feelings</strong> without anything objective behind them.",r:"Defenders say that only shows where our feelings come from, not whether they track truth. Critics say that if the origin is blind, we should trust the feelings less."}]},
 e:{ev:["Realists appeal to moral intuition as a source of knowledge, to the Moorean point that we are more certain of some moral truths than of any premise in a sceptical argument, to moral progress, and to the apparently irreducible normativity of reasons (Parfit; Enoch's indispensability argument)."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Error theory and debunking.</strong> Mackie (<em>Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong</em>, 1977) argues objective values would be metaphysically 'queer'. Street's Darwinian dilemma (2006) argues that evolution leaves it unexplained why our moral beliefs would match any independent moral truth. Joyce develops an evolutionary debunking case for error theory.",r:"Realists reply with a 'third factor' account on which survival-promoting beliefs happen to track moral truth (Enoch), or deny the dilemma. Some theists add that God plus evolution explains the match, which critics see as a rival escape rather than an argument."}],
    who:"Defend: Parfit, Shafer-Landau, Enoch, Craig. Dispute: Mackie, Street, Joyce."}},

m_pp1:{plain:"If God is what makes morals real, God is the perfect example of goodness.",
 s:{ev:["If goodness comes from God, then God is the standard of good itself, not just someone who happens to follow the rules."],
    ob:[{t:"Is God good because of a standard outside God?",r:"The idea is that the standard is God's own nature, so there is nothing outside."}]},
 i:{ev:["The theistic answer to Euthyphro is that God's nature is the paradigm of goodness: God is not measured against goodness, God is what goodness is."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Is 'God is good' then empty?</strong> If goodness just means 'like God', the claim is trivial.",r:"Defenders say goodness is a real property that God has perfectly; the paradigm is how we fix the reference, not the whole meaning."}]},
 e:{ev:["Adams: the Good is identified with God (the transcendent Good), and obligations with the commands of a loving God. Alston and Craig defend versions that locate the standard in God's nature to avoid both horns of Euthyphro."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Arbitrariness and triviality.</strong> Wielenberg and Parfit argue that a nature-based standard is either arbitrary (why this nature?) or makes 'God is good' trivial.",r:"Defenders say a necessary, essentially perfect nature is neither arbitrary nor contingent; critics say that restates the problem."}],
    who:"Defend: Adams, Alston, Craig. Dispute: Wielenberg, Parfit."}},

m_pp2:{plain:"Duties are owed to someone, so the source of morality has to be a person.",
 s:{ev:["A duty is something you owe, and commands come from someone. So the thing behind moral duties looks like a person, not just a rule."],
    ob:[{t:"Maybe moral rules are just facts, like maths, and need no person.",r:"That is the main rival view. Defenders say facts alone do not explain why you <em>ought</em> to do anything."}]},
 i:{ev:["Moral duties feel like they are owed and have authority over us, which fits a personal source, a loving authority that cares about us, better than an impersonal rule."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Impersonal moral facts</strong> might have authority without a person.",r:"Defenders say authority without an authority is puzzling; critics say reasons are like that."}]},
 e:{ev:["Argument from the second-personal character of moral obligation (Darwall) is used by theists such as Evans and Zagzebski: obligations are owed to persons and so are best grounded in a personal being whose commands generate them."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Non-personal normativity.</strong> Parfit and Enoch defend irreducibly normative facts that are not commands; Darwall himself develops second-personal morality without theism.",r:"Defenders say impersonal normativity cannot explain accountability; critics say accountability can be grounded in persons without a divine one."}],
    who:"Defend: Evans, Zagzebski. Dispute: Parfit, Enoch, Darwall (secular version)."}},

m_pp3:{plain:"Moral truths do not change with the universe, so their source must be beyond it.",
 s:{ev:["Right and wrong would still be right and wrong even if the universe were different. So their source cannot be just part of the universe."],
    ob:[{t:"Maybe morals are tied to how humans are, and that is part of the universe.",r:"That would make morals depend on us, which is the opposite of objective."}]},
 i:{ev:["Objective moral truths seem necessary and not dependent on physical facts, so their ground must be a necessary reality beyond the contingent physical world."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Abstract moral facts could be necessary themselves.</strong>",r:"Defenders say abstract facts cannot ground duties or goodness without a concrete being, which critics dispute."}]},
 e:{ev:["Moral facts, if objective and necessary, cannot be grounded in contingent physical facts; theistic Platonism (Adams) and divine-nature theories (Alston) place the ground in a necessary being."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Naturalistic and constructivist accounts.</strong> Some argue moral facts are natural facts (Railton, Cornell realism) or construction from rational agency (Korsgaard); both avoid a transcendent ground.",r:"Defenders argue these accounts lose objectivity or necessity; critics say they preserve enough of both."}],
    who:"Defend: Adams, Alston. Dispute: Railton, Korsgaard."}},

/* ===== CONTINGENCY ===== */
c_p1:{plain:"Everything that exists has a reason why it exists.",
 s:{ev:["We expect a reason for why things exist. Either something else caused them, or they have to exist because of what they are, like 2 + 2 = 4."],
    ob:[{t:"Why must everything have a reason? Maybe some things just are.",r:"People really do disagree about this. But if anything could just be, the usual search for reasons in science and daily life would be on shaky ground."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>The Principle of Sufficient Reason.</strong> Leibniz held that nothing is without a reason. It matches how we reason in science and daily life: if we find an unexplained object, we look for the explanation rather than accept there isn't one.","<strong>Two kinds of reason.</strong> Something is explained either by another thing (a cause) or by its own nature (it cannot fail to exist)."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Maybe the universe is a brute fact.</strong> Russell said the universe is just there.",r:"Defenders say that gives up on explanation exactly where it matters most. Critics say it is the honest stopping point."},{t:"<strong>If everything has an explanation, everything is necessary.</strong> (Modal collapse)",r:"Defenders (Pruss, Koons) say weaker versions of the principle avoid this. Critics are not convinced."}]},
 e:{ev:["Pruss (<em>The Principle of Sufficient Reason: A Reassessment</em>, 2006) defends the PSR from the practice of inquiry, from its apparent self-evidence and from the difficulty of restricting it without counterexamples. Gale and Pruss (1999) and Koons (2008) build cosmological arguments on weakened versions."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Modal collapse and brute facts.</strong> Van Inwagen (1983) argued a strong PSR implies that every truth is necessary. Della Rocca defends a thoroughgoing PSR and accepts that Spinozist consequence. Russell (in his 1948 debate with Copleston), Hume (<em>Dialogues</em>, Part IX) and Oppy hold that a brute-fact universe is acceptable.",r:"Pruss and Koons argue that restricted PSRs and a distinction between kinds of explanation avoid collapse. Oppy says such restrictions are unmotivated."}],
    who:"Defend: Pruss, Koons, Leibniz. Dispute: Van Inwagen, Oppy, Russell, Hume (Philo)."}},

c_p2:{plain:"The universe could have not existed. It is not the kind of thing that has to exist.",
 s:{ev:["Nothing about the universe says it had to exist. We can easily imagine there being no stars, planets or space at all, so it looks like it could have failed to exist."],
    ob:[{t:"Maybe the universe has to exist and we do not understand why.",r:"Possible. But then we would expect to find some reason it could not be otherwise."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Everything in it is contingent.</strong> Each particle, star and planet could have been otherwise. A collection of contingent things looks contingent too.","<strong>No sign of necessity.</strong> The laws and starting conditions could have been different, which a necessary reality would not allow."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Fallacy of composition.</strong> Parts being contingent does not prove the whole is.",r:"Defenders say that if every part could fail to exist and the whole is just those parts, the whole depends on them. Critics say this is not guaranteed."}]},
 e:{ev:["The universe's constituents, laws and initial conditions appear modally contingent. Pruss and Gale use a 'Big Conjunctive Contingent Fact' (BCCF) formulation, the conjunction of all contingent truths, which avoids inferring a property of the whole from its parts."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Composition and necessary laws.</strong> Russell and Hume press the composition point. Dispositional essentialists (Bird, Ellis) hold that laws are metaphysically necessary, which would reduce what is contingent.",r:"Defenders note that initial conditions would remain contingent even on necessary laws, and that the BCCF version does not rest on a composition inference."}],
    who:"Defend: Leibniz, Clarke, Rowe, Pruss. Dispute: Hume (Philo), Russell, Oppy, Bird."}},

c_pp1:{plain:"A necessary being is where every chain of explanation ends.",
 s:{ev:["If everything that depends on something else traces back to one thing that does not depend on anything, that thing is the 'first' cause of all of it."],
    ob:[{t:"Is 'first' about time, or about dependence?",r:"About dependence. The necessary being would hold everything up now, not just start it long ago."}]},
 i:{ev:["Contingent things depend on something else for their existence at every moment. The explanation has to bottom out in a being that depends on nothing, a source of everything contingent."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Why only one?</strong> There could be several necessary things.",r:"Defenders use simplicity to favour one; critics say that is not a proof."}]},
 e:{ev:["On Leibnizian arguments, the explanation of the BCCF must be a necessary being, and a necessary being that explains it is, in effect, the source of contingent reality (Pruss; Gale &amp; Pruss)."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Is the necessary being the universe itself?</strong> Some (Spinoza-style, or Oppy's discussion of necessitarianism) take a necessary physical reality as a candidate.",r:"Defenders argue that a physical reality full of contingent parts cannot be necessary, so the explanation must lie outside it."}],
    who:"Defend: Pruss, Gale. Dispute: Oppy."}},

c_pp2:{plain:"A necessary being cannot be part of the changeable physical world.",
 s:{ev:["The physical world is full of things that change and might have been different. A being that has to exist cannot be one of those."],
    ob:[{t:"Maybe the basic laws of physics are the necessary thing.",r:"They might be, but then you would need to explain why those laws and not others."}]},
 i:{ev:["Physical things are contingent: they could have been otherwise. A necessary being is therefore non-physical, something beyond physical reality."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Physical laws could be necessary</strong> and do the explaining.",r:"Defenders say laws describe what matter does but do not explain why anything exists."}]},
 e:{ev:["If the explanatory terminus of contingent physical reality must be non-contingent, and physical reality as ordinarily understood is contingent, the terminus is transcendent of it."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Necessary physical stuff?</strong> Necessitarian views of laws and of fundamental physical stuff (Spinoza; dispositional essentialism) allow a necessary, physical terminus.",r:"Defenders reply that this threatens the contingency of everything else, so those views carry the modal-collapse cost."}],
    who:"Dispute: Spinozists, dispositional essentialists."}},

c_pp3:{plain:"The best explanation of why anything contingent exists is a mind that chose to create it.",
 s:{ev:["Why does anything contingent exist, when it might not? A mind that chooses to make things is a good answer. A blind necessity would just produce everything it can, all the time."],
    ob:[{t:"Why would the necessary thing be a mind and not just a force?",r:"That is the open question. This step says it is the most plausible, not that it is certain."}]},
 i:{ev:["A necessary being that produces contingent things needs a way to explain why <em>these</em> things and not others. Free choice by a mind explains that. An impersonal necessity would not."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>The choice might not be needed.</strong> A necessary being might just give rise to contingent things impersonally.",r:"Critics say then everything would follow necessarily. Defenders say that is the problem: the contingent would not really be contingent."}]},
 e:{ev:["Pruss and Koons argue that a free personal cause best explains the contingent: a necessary impersonal cause would transmit necessity to its effects (a collapse of contingency), whereas a free agent can explain a contingent effect without necessitating it."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Does a free choice explain anything?</strong> Oppy and Rowe query whether 'a free choice' is any better than a brute contingent fact, and Hume's Philo asks why mind should be the ultimate explainer.",r:"Defenders argue that agent explanation is a basic explanatory type already used in ordinary life."}],
    who:"Defend: Pruss, Koons. Dispute: Oppy, Rowe, Hume (Philo)."}},

/* ===== ONTOLOGICAL ===== */
o_p1:{plain:"There is no hidden contradiction in the idea of a perfect, all-powerful being, so it could exist.",
 s:{ev:["We can understand the idea of a being that is all-powerful, all-knowing and perfectly good. Nobody has found a hidden contradiction in it, so it looks possible."],
    ob:[{t:"Being able to imagine something does not mean it is possible.",r:"True. Defenders say we should count it as possible unless someone shows a problem. Critics say that is the whole argument."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>No contradiction found.</strong> Defenders say the concept of a being with every perfection is coherent, and that the burden is on those who say it is impossible.","<strong>It is a modest claim.</strong> 'Possible' only means 'not ruled out'."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>It quietly assumes the conclusion.</strong> If God does not exist, a necessary being is impossible, so granting that God is possible is a lot to ask.",r:"This is the main complaint. Defenders say we have no reason to treat the concept as impossible."},{t:"<strong>Parodies.</strong> The same trick seems to 'prove' a perfect island or a maximally evil being.",r:"Defenders say those are not coherent in the same way. Critics say parodies can be built that fit."}]},
 e:{ev:["<strong>Plantinga's version (<em>The Nature of Necessity</em>, 1974).</strong> A being is maximally great if it has maximal excellence (omnipotence, omniscience, moral perfection) in every possible world. Premise one says such a being exists in some possible world. Plantinga himself presented the argument as showing that belief in God is rationally acceptable rather than as a proof, since premise one cannot be established by neutral means.","<strong>Formal variants.</strong> G&ouml;del's version (notes from 1970, published 1995) and Maydole's modal variants try to derive the possibility claim from axioms about positive properties."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Possibility and question-begging.</strong> Oppy (<em>Ontological Arguments and Belief in God</em>, 1995) argues the argument fails to be rationally persuasive: anyone who doubts God's existence has equal reason to doubt the possibility premise. Parody arguments (Gaunilo and later) suggest the premise cannot be accepted on logical grounds alone.",r:"Plantinga and Maydole defend the premise through modal intuition and through consistency proofs, though G&ouml;del-style systems face the objection that they collapse modal distinctions (Sobel, 1987)."}],
    who:"Defend: Plantinga, Maydole, G&ouml;del (formal version). Dispute: Oppy, Sobel, Gaunilo (parody), Kant."}},

o_p2:{plain:"If it is possible that a perfect being exists, and perfection includes existing necessarily, then it does exist.",
 s:{ev:["A perfect being would exist in every possible world, not just some, because existing only sometimes is not perfect. So if it exists in even one possible world, it exists in all of them, including ours."],
    ob:[{t:"This is word-magic. You cannot define something into existence.",r:"That is the classic worry. Defenders say the logic is valid and the real issue is whether you accept that the being is possible."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>The logic is valid.</strong> If 'necessary existence' means existing in all possible worlds, and the being exists in at least one, it exists in all, including ours (using the modal logic called S5).","<strong>Necessity is part of greatness.</strong> A being that could fail to exist would be less great."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Existence is not a property (Kant).</strong> You cannot add 'exists' to a concept to make it real.",r:"Modern defenders say the argument does not treat existence as an ordinary property but uses necessary existence and possible worlds."},{t:"<strong>It depends on a particular modal logic (S5).</strong>",r:"S5 is widely used for metaphysical possibility, but some philosophers doubt it for this case."}]},
 e:{ev:["The step uses the S5 principle that what is possibly necessary is necessary (&#9674;&#9633;P &rarr; &#9633;P) so that, together with premise one, the being exists in every world including the actual one. Plantinga, Hartshorne (in his modal version) and Malcolm (1960) hold that this is the correct logic of metaphysical modality, or at least that its use here is plausible."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Which kind of possibility, and which logic?</strong> Oppy argues that the possibility in premise one may be merely epistemic ('for all we know') and that moving from epistemic to metaphysical possibility is unwarranted. Salmon rejects S5 for metaphysical modality.",r:"Defenders reply that metaphysical possibility is the intended sense and that a necessary being's possibility has independent support, but this puts the burden back on premise one."}],
    who:"Defend: Plantinga, Hartshorne, Malcolm. Dispute: Oppy, Salmon, Kant."}},

o_pp1:{plain:"A being that must exist and has every perfection is the God of classical theism.",
 s:{ev:["The argument is about a being with every good quality in the highest degree and that has to exist. That is what many religions mean by God."],
    ob:[{t:"The argument does not say this being is the God of any one religion.",r:"True. It only gets as far as a perfect, necessary being."}]},
 i:{ev:["A maximally great being has all the 'omni' qualities, power, knowledge and goodness, and exists necessarily. This lines up with classical theism."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Do all the perfections fit together?</strong> Some say omnipotence and perfect goodness or omniscience and freedom conflict.",r:"If they conflict, the concept is impossible and the first premise fails. Defenders say refined definitions avoid the conflicts."}]},
 e:{ev:["Maximal greatness (Plantinga) entails maximal excellence in every world: omnipotence, omniscience and moral perfection, and so connects to the God of classical theism, though not to any particular revelation."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Coherence of the package.</strong> Arguments of incoherence (Mackie; Martin; Oppy's survey in <em>Arguing about Gods</em>) press the case that omniscience, omnipotence, perfect goodness and timelessness conflict.",r:"Defenders (Plantinga; Swinburne; Stump &amp; Kretzmann) offer refined analyses, and each such argument targets premise one."}],
    who:"Defend: Plantinga, Swinburne. Dispute: Mackie, Martin, Oppy."}},

/* ===== RESURRECTION ===== */
r_p1:{plain:"Most historians agree Jesus was crucified and that his followers soon sincerely believed they had seen him alive. Some of the other 'facts' are more disputed.",
 s:{ev:["Most historians agree that Jesus was crucified and that soon after, his followers sincerely believed they had seen him alive. Many were ready to suffer for saying so. The question is what explains that."],
    ob:[{t:"Some of the 'facts', like the empty tomb, are disputed by historians.",r:"True. Defenders say most scholars accept them for good reasons. Critics say the evidence is weaker than that."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>The 'minimal facts' method.</strong> Habermas and Licona pick facts accepted by most scholars, including sceptics: Jesus died by crucifixion, his followers had experiences they took to be appearances of him, and the early church was transformed.","<strong>Early evidence.</strong> Paul's list of appearances in 1 Corinthians 15 is dated by most scholars to within a few years of the events.","<strong>The empty tomb</strong> is supported by the early burial tradition (Joseph of Arimathea) and by the early argument over <em>why</em> the tomb was empty."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Reliability of the Gospels.</strong> They were written decades later by believers.",r:"Defenders say they are early enough and based on eyewitness tradition. Critics say legend grows quickly."},{t:"<strong>The burial and empty tomb are doubted.</strong> Crucified bodies were often not buried properly.",r:"Defenders cite the early burial tradition. Critics (Crossan, Ehrman) say it is a later invention."}]},
 e:{ev:["<strong>Minimal facts.</strong> Habermas and Licona (<em>The Case for the Resurrection of Jesus</em>, 2004) restrict themselves to data accepted by the large majority of critical scholars: the crucifixion, early appearance experiences, the conversion of Paul and of James, and the disciples' transformation, with the empty tomb argued to be accepted by a majority. N. T. Wright (<em>The Resurrection of the Son of God</em>, 2003) argues the combination of an empty tomb and bodily appearances best explains the rise of Christian belief within Second Temple Jewish categories.","<strong>Early tradition.</strong> Most scholars date the creed in 1 Cor 15:3&ndash;7 to within a few years of the crucifixion (Paul says he 'received' it). Allison (<em>Resurrecting Jesus</em>, 2005) examines the evidence carefully while declining to settle the explanation."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Contested facts and method.</strong> Ehrman argues burial in a tomb by Joseph of Arimathea is historically unlikely given Roman practice, that the empty tomb first appears in Mark, and that the accounts diverge. L&uuml;demann (<em>The Resurrection of Jesus</em>, 1994) treats the appearances as visionary experiences, and Carrier (<em>Proving History</em>, 2012) disputes the criteria of authenticity used.",r:"Defenders reply that the burial story has multiple lines of attestation and fits Jewish burial practice, and that divergence in appearance accounts is expected in independent testimony. The disputes are largely about method and how much weight to give Mark and Paul."}],
    who:"Defend: Habermas, Licona, Wright, Craig. Dispute: Ehrman, L&uuml;demann, Carrier, Crossan. Careful: Allison."}},

r_p2:{plain:"Given those facts, the best explanation is that God raised Jesus.",
 s:{ev:["The disciples were convinced they had seen Jesus alive after he was killed. Defenders say the simplest explanation is that he really was raised, because other ideas, like hallucinations, do not easily explain all the facts together."],
    ob:[{t:"Miracles are far less likely than other explanations, such as visions, legends or mistakes.",r:"This is the central dispute. Defenders say that if God exists a resurrection is not so unlikely. Critics say that assumes what is being proved."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Inference to the best explanation.</strong> A good explanation covers all the facts, is plausible and is not overcomplicated. Defenders argue that rivals (hallucination, swoon, theft, legend) each fail to explain one or more of the facts.","<strong>An unexpected idea.</strong> In Jewish thought resurrection was a general event at the end of time, so a single man being raised first was surprising, which makes a pure-invention story less likely."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Hume on miracles.</strong> We should always find a natural explanation more likely than a miracle.",r:"Defenders say Hume's argument assumes there are no miracles. Critics say the prior probability of a miracle remains very low."},{t:"<strong>Other explanations may fit.</strong> Grief visions, legendary growth or later invention.",r:"Defenders say these struggle to explain group experiences and the speed of the belief. Critics say they fit well enough."}]},
 e:{ev:["<strong>Bayesian and explanatory cases.</strong> Swinburne (<em>The Resurrection of God Incarnate</em>, 2003) argues that, given a reasonable prior for an incarnation from natural theology and the moral significance of Jesus' life, the evidence makes the resurrection probable. McGrew and McGrew defend a related Bayesian argument. Craig defends an inference to the best explanation, comparing rivals on explanatory scope and power, plausibility and how ad hoc they are."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Priors and Hume.</strong> Earman (<em>Hume's Abject Failure</em>, 2000) rejects Hume's in-principle argument but concludes that testimony to miracles is typically too weak to overcome very low priors. Ehrman argues that historians are limited to the most probable natural explanation. Carrier argues that realistic reference classes give a very low prior.",r:"Swinburne and the McGrews dispute the low prior by appealing to background theistic evidence. Critics reply that the question then depends on how strong the arguments for God are, which is what this tool lets you set."}],
    who:"Defend: Swinburne, McGrew, Craig, Wright. Dispute: Earman, Ehrman, Carrier."}},

r_pp1:{plain:"If God raised Jesus, then the God Jesus preached about exists.",
 s:{ev:["If the resurrection really happened, it would be an act of God, and Jesus taught about the God of Israel. So that God would be shown to exist."],
    ob:[{t:"Maybe some other power raised him.",r:"Then the claims about who that power is would need more evidence."}]},
 i:{ev:["A resurrection would be a divine endorsement of Jesus and his message. Since Jesus taught about the God of Israel, the God raised him by acting would be that God."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Other religions have miracle claims too.</strong>",r:"Defenders say the evidence for each must be weighed on its own; critics say this makes miracle evidence cancel out."}]},
 e:{ev:["Swinburne argues that a resurrection, in the context of Jesus' life and teaching, would be a divine signature on his claims. The inference to specifically Christian doctrine then relies on the content of that teaching."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Cancellation and pluralism.</strong> Hume's 'cancellation' argument in <em>Of Miracles</em> holds that rival miracle claims weaken each other, and Hick-style pluralism suggests the same divine reality could be behind many traditions.",r:"Defenders reply that miracle reports differ greatly in quality and in the doctrines they support, so cancellation is not automatic."}],
    who:"Defend: Swinburne. Dispute: Hume, Hick."}},

/* ===== LIAR, LUNATIC, LORD ===== */
lll_p1:{plain:"Jesus said and did things that his listeners understood as claiming to be God.",
 s:{ev:["In the Gospels Jesus forgives sins, accepts worship and says things like 'before Abraham was, I am'. People around him treated this as claiming to be God."],
    ob:[{t:"The Gospels were written later, so he may never have said these things.",r:"This is the big question. Many scholars think the strongest claims, in John, were shaped by the early church."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Actions and words.</strong> Forgiving sins, speaking with personal authority ('but I say to you') and the 'I am' sayings in John. In the accounts, his opponents charge him with blasphemy.","<strong>Early worship.</strong> Early Christians worshipped Jesus within a few years, which suggests the idea did not come from nowhere (Hurtado)."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>The later Gospels, especially John, may reflect later belief</strong> and not his own words.",r:"Defenders point to claims in the earlier Gospels too. Critics (Ehrman) say the explicit divine claims appear only late."},{t:"<strong>Jewish ideas like 'Son of God' or 'Messiah'</strong> did not mean being God.",r:"Defenders say Jesus' claims went further. Critics say they stay within Jewish categories."}]},
 e:{ev:["Bauckham (<em>Jesus and the Identity of God</em>, 2008) argues early Christology included Jesus within the unique divine identity of Israel's monotheism. Hurtado (<em>Lord Jesus Christ</em>, 2003) argues that devotion to Jesus as divine appeared remarkably early. Craig Evans and others defend an implicit divine self-understanding in the Synoptic sayings (authority over Torah, sin and Sabbath)."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Historical Jesus scholarship.</strong> Ehrman (<em>How Jesus Became God</em>, 2014) argues that Jesus did not claim divinity and that the idea developed after his death. Vermes (<em>Jesus the Jew</em>, 1973) reads his titles in a Jewish prophetic context. The Synoptics and John differ strongly on explicit claims.",r:"Bauckham and Hurtado argue that 'divine identity' Christology is early and Jewish. Critics respond that this shows early belief, not Jesus' own claim."}],
    who:"Defend: Bauckham, Hurtado, Evans. Dispute: Ehrman, Vermes."}},

lll_p2:{plain:"C. S. Lewis argued there are only three options: Jesus lied, was mistaken, or told the truth.",
 s:{ev:["C. S. Lewis said that someone who claims to be God is either lying, mad, or telling the truth. He argued you cannot just call such a person a 'great moral teacher'."],
    ob:[{t:"There are more options than that, for example that the claims were added later or misunderstood.",r:"Yes, and that is the biggest criticism of the argument."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>The trilemma</strong> (Lewis, <em>Mere Christianity</em>). If Jesus claimed to be God, 'great moral teacher' is not available as a middle option: such a person is deceiving, deluded or right."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>The trilemma ignores other options</strong>, such as legend, misreporting or a metaphorical claim.",r:"Defenders say the trilemma only applies once the first premise is established. Critics say that puts too much weight on that premise."}]},
 e:{ev:["The trilemma is a disjunction that presupposes the divine claims are historical, as popularised by Lewis and defended by Kreeft and Tacelli (<em>Handbook of Christian Apologetics</em>, 1994). It is best read as an argument to be combined with other evidence, not a knock-down proof."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Incomplete disjunction.</strong> Beversluis (<em>C. S. Lewis and the Search for Rational Religion</em>, 1985) argued the trilemma is inadequate because it ignores legend and misreporting by the disciples. Ehrman and others hold that it rests on a naive reading of the Gospels.",r:"Defenders reply that if the claims are historical (premise one), legend is excluded for those sayings, so the weight rests on that premise."}],
    who:"Defend: Lewis, Kreeft, Tacelli. Dispute: Beversluis, Ehrman."}},

lll_p3:{plain:"Jesus' life and teaching do not look like those of a liar or a madman.",
 s:{ev:["The Jesus of the Gospels is wise, humble and calm. That does not fit a liar or a person with delusions."],
    ob:[{t:"A sincere person can still be wrong, and wise people can hold odd beliefs.",r:"True. Defenders say being wrong about being God would be an extreme mistake for someone otherwise so sane."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Not a liar.</strong> His teaching centres on honesty and he died for his claims.","<strong>Not deluded.</strong> The Gospels show composure, compassion and sharp reasoning, unlike the pattern of serious mental illness."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Dying for a belief shows sincerity, not truth.</strong>",r:"Defenders agree it shows sincerity, which is all the 'not a liar' step needs."},{t:"<strong>Diagnosing someone from ancient texts is unreliable.</strong>",r:"Defenders concede this. Critics say it undermines the 'not deluded' step."}]},
 e:{ev:["The case relies on the moral and psychological coherence of the Gospel portrait (Lewis; Kreeft and Tacelli; Craig) and the claim that grandiose claims in other historical figures come with instability rather than wisdom."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Apocalyptic prophet.</strong> Schweitzer, Sanders and Ehrman portray Jesus as a first-century apocalyptic prophet who may have been mistaken about the imminent kingdom: a sincere and impressive person who nevertheless held false beliefs.",r:"Defenders say being mistaken about timing is different from being mistaken about being divine. Critics say it shows high moral character is compatible with serious error."}],
    who:"Defend: Lewis, Kreeft, Craig. Dispute: Schweitzer, Sanders, Ehrman."}},

lll_pp1:{plain:"If Jesus really is who he said he was, then the God of Christianity exists.",
 s:{ev:["If Jesus really was the Son of God, then the God he talked about is real."],
    ob:[{t:"That only follows if the earlier steps all work.",r:"Yes. This step is just 'if the rest is true, then this follows'."}]},
 i:{ev:["Jesus' divine claims identify him with the God of Israel. If true, they establish that God, and Christianity's central claim about Jesus."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>It adds nothing to the earlier steps.</strong> It is just the conclusion restated.",r:"Defenders agree it is a conditional: its role is to connect the earlier conclusion to the Christian God."}]},
 e:{ev:["Given that the Gospel portrait's claims are accurate and Jesus is who he claimed, the God of Israel whom he called Father exists, and this would be the Christian God of the creeds (including the Trinitarian developments argued by Bauckham and Hurtado to be present in early devotion)."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Doctrinal gap.</strong> Even if Jesus held a unique role, the step from there to Nicene Trinitarian doctrine is a later development (Ehrman; Dunn).",r:"Defenders argue that Nicaea articulated what was implicit; critics see it as a development of the tradition."}],
    who:"Defend: Bauckham, Hurtado. Dispute: Ehrman, Dunn."}},

/* ===== SHROUD ===== */
sh_p1:{plain:"The Shroud of Turin is a cloth with the faint image of a crucified man. Whether it is Jesus' real burial cloth is one of the most disputed claims in this tool.",
 s:{ev:["The cloth shows a faint image of a man who looks crucified, with wounds like the Gospel story. Some researchers think the image is too strange to be a painting."],
    ob:[{t:"In 1988 three labs radiocarbon-dated it to about 1260&ndash;1390 AD, over a thousand years after Jesus.",r:"Defenders say the sample may have come from a medieval repair, and some dispute the result. Most scientists accept the dating."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Image properties.</strong> The 1978 STURP study found no pigment in the image areas, and the image carries 3D-like information.","<strong>Wounds.</strong> Supporters say the marks match crucifixion and the Gospel details (lance wound, scourging, crown of thorns).","<strong>Challenges to the dating.</strong> Ray Rogers argued the dated sample was from a medieval repair, and some statistical re-analyses question how uniform the results were."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Radiocarbon dating.</strong> Three labs (Oxford, Zurich, Arizona; <em>Nature</em>, 1989) dated the cloth to 1260&ndash;1390.",r:"Defenders say the sample was unrepresentative. Critics reply that it was taken from a main part of the cloth and the repair theory has not been confirmed."},{t:"<strong>History.</strong> The documented history starts in the 1350s, and a 1389 bishop's letter says an artist had admitted making it.",r:"Defenders point to earlier traces (such as the Image of Edessa), which critics call speculative."},{t:"<strong>Reproductions.</strong> Garlaschelli produced a similar image with medieval methods.",r:"Defenders say these miss some properties. Critics say they come close enough."}]},
 e:{ev:["<strong>The case for authenticity.</strong> Proponents (Jackson, Antonacci, Fanti) cite the STURP findings (1978), the 3D encoding of the image, the apparent absence of pigment or brush marks, the claimed blood and anatomical correlations with scourging and crucifixion, and the argument that the 1988 sampling was unrepresentative: Rogers (2005) reported chemical differences at the sampling corner. Other physical dating methods have been reported to give much older dates, though these are disputed."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Dating and provenance.</strong> The 1988 radiocarbon dates (Damon et al., <em>Nature</em>, 1989) were 1260&ndash;1390 CE at 95% confidence. A 2019 re-analysis of the raw data (Casabianca et al.) reported heterogeneity that questions the stated precision, though it did not itself establish a first-century date. Documented history begins in the 1350s, and the 1389 d'Arcis memorandum reports an artist's confession. McCrone and Nickell argued the image is pigment and medieval technique, and Garlaschelli (2009) reproduced a comparable image.",r:"Proponents contest McCrone's identification and the sampling. Most mainstream scientists and historians still accept a medieval date, so treat this as a premise on which scholarly opinion is strongly against authenticity."}],
    who:"Defend: Jackson, Antonacci, Fanti, Rogers (sampling critique). Dispute: Damon et al., Nickell, McCrone, Garlaschelli."}},

sh_p2:{plain:"Some believers say a burst of light from inside the body made the image. Most scientists do not accept this.",
 s:{ev:["Some researchers say the image is too faint and too shallow in the cloth to be paint, and that a burst of radiation is one idea to explain it."],
    ob:[{t:"Nobody has seen or made such a burst, and ordinary methods can copy the image.",r:"Defenders say those copies miss details. Critics say this jumps from 'unexplained' to 'resurrection'."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Superficial image.</strong> The image sits only on the very top fibres, which is hard to match with liquid paint.","<strong>No brush strokes.</strong> The image does not show the marks a painter would leave.","<strong>Radiation experiments.</strong> Some experiments with UV light produced faint, surface-level colouring on linen."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>A gap in the argument.</strong> Even if the image is hard to explain, 'unknown' is not the same as 'resurrection'.",r:"This is a fair point against the argument. Defenders say the radiation idea is a hypothesis for the best explanation."},{t:"<strong>Natural methods may work.</strong> Experiments with rubbing and chemical processes produce similar images.",r:"Defenders say none reproduces every property at once."}]},
 e:{ev:["Proponents (Jackson's cloth-collapse hypothesis; Di Lazzaro and colleagues at ENEA, who produced superficial colouring of linen with vacuum-ultraviolet laser pulses) argue that no known contact, vapour or painting process reproduces all the image features together, and propose a brief burst of radiation as a working hypothesis."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Scale and testability.</strong> The ENEA authors themselves note that the light energy needed to colour a whole body-sized image exceeds what existing sources can deliver, and they reproduced only some features. A resurrection explanation is not independently testable, and this premise inherits the problems of P1: without a first-century date the inference fails.",r:"Defenders reply that the difficulty of natural replication keeps the question open. Most scholars treat the image as a medieval artefact."}],
    who:"Defend: Jackson, Di Lazzaro (as hypothesis). Dispute: Nickell, Garlaschelli, mainstream historians."}},

sh_pp1:{plain:"If the Shroud really showed a resurrection, that would point to the God Christians believe in.",
 s:{ev:["If the physical evidence really confirmed a resurrection, that would be strong evidence for the God of Christianity."],
    ob:[{t:"That only works if the Shroud is real, and the evidence is against that.",r:"Yes. This step is only 'if it were true, then this follows'."}]},
 i:{ev:["A physical sign of resurrection would support the claim that God raised Jesus. This links to the resurrection argument."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>It depends on the first two steps</strong>, which are the weakest in this argument.",r:"Agreed. It is a conditional."}]},
 e:{ev:["Conditional on both premises, the Shroud would be physical corroboration of the Gospel resurrection claim, and so would feed into the resurrection argument above."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Non-independence.</strong> This argument is not independent of the resurrection argument, so treating both as separate sources of evidence double counts. The tool's noisy-OR combination assumes independence, so rate carefully.",r:"Defenders say the Shroud would be independent physical evidence; critics note that its force depends on already accepting the resurrection claim."}],
    who:"Dispute: critics of double counting."}},

/* ===== PROPERLY BASIC BELIEFS ===== */
pb_p1:{plain:"Everyone lives as if meaning, love, right and wrong, dignity and free will are real, even if they cannot prove it.",
 s:{ev:["Almost everyone acts as if some things are really true: that people matter, that love is real, that cruelty is wrong and that we make real choices. We cannot prove these things, but we cannot help living by them."],
    ob:[{t:"Not everyone believes in free will or objective meaning, and people change their minds.",r:"Fair. Defenders say that even people who deny them keep living as if they were true."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Properly basic beliefs.</strong> Philosophers such as Plantinga argue that some beliefs are rational without argument, like trusting memory or other minds, and that belief in moral truth and human dignity belongs in the same group.","<strong>Practically inescapable.</strong> Even those who deny them in theory find it very hard to live that way."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Does 'inescapable' mean 'true'?</strong> Evolution may explain why we cannot help believing.",r:"This is the core worry. Defenders say that if a belief is basic and inescapable, we should trust it unless given a good reason not to."},{t:"<strong>The Great Pumpkin objection.</strong> If any belief can be 'basic', so can absurd ones.",r:"Defenders say basicality depends on the right circumstances and on defeaters. Critics say that makes it hard to tell which beliefs qualify."}]},
 e:{ev:["<strong>Reformed epistemology.</strong> Plantinga (<em>Reason and Belief in God</em>, 1983; <em>Warranted Christian Belief</em>, 2000) argues that beliefs can be warranted without propositional evidence when produced by properly functioning faculties in a suitable environment. Alston (<em>Perceiving God</em>, 1991) defends a parallel for religious experience, and the common-sense tradition of Reid supports taking natural convictions at face value."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Great Pumpkin and defeaters.</strong> The 'Great Pumpkin objection' (discussed by Plantinga himself and pressed by Martin and others) asks what stops any belief from being 'properly basic'. Evolutionary debunking (Street; Joyce) offers a potential defeater for moral and similar beliefs. And several of the claimed beliefs, such as libertarian free will, are not in fact universal.",r:"Plantinga replies that basicality is assessed within a community of belief and that defeaters must be argued for, not assumed. Critics say this makes the epistemology unfalsifiable."}],
    who:"Defend: Plantinga, Alston, Reid. Dispute: Martin, Street, Joyce."}},

pb_p2:{plain:"If these things are real, not just comfortable stories, then the universe must be more than matter and physics.",
 s:{ev:["Atoms and forces do not obviously contain meaning, love or rights. If those are real, there must be more to reality than physical stuff."],
    ob:[{t:"Meaning and love can be real as things happening in brains, without being 'built into' the universe.",r:"That is the main alternative. Defenders say it makes them less than objective."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>The gap.</strong> Science describes what is, but not obviously what ought to be, what is worthy or why anything matters.","<strong>Naturalism's options.</strong> Naturalists can try to reduce these to physical facts, explain them away, or accept that they are useful fictions."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Reductive and emergent accounts</strong> try to place value and meaning in the natural world.",r:"Defenders say these either change the meaning of the terms or fail to make them objective."},{t:"<strong>Non-theistic non-naturalism</strong> (such as Parfit's moral realism) holds them real without God.",r:"That points to the next premise's question: can anything but God ground them?"}]},
 e:{ev:["Defenders (Plantinga; Craig) argue that naturalism in its standard form, a causally closed physical world explained by natural science, lacks the resources for irreducible normativity, intentionality, value and libertarian freedom. Plantinga's evolutionary argument against naturalism (<em>Warrant and Proper Function</em>, 1993) targets the reliability of our cognitive faculties themselves."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Naturalism is not a single view.</strong> Cornell realists (Boyd, Sturgeon, Railton) offer natural moral facts, and compatibilists reject the libertarian view of freedom. Fitelson and Sober (1998) criticise Plantinga's evolutionary argument on probabilistic grounds. Nagel (<em>Mind and Cosmos</em>, 2012) argues that reductive naturalism fails without endorsing theism.",r:"Defenders argue these views redefine the target features. Critics say they capture what matters about them."}],
    who:"Defend: Plantinga, Craig. Dispute: Boyd, Sturgeon, Railton, Fitelson &amp; Sober."}},

pb_p3:{plain:"Only a good, loving mind behind everything, in other words God, can make sense of why those things are real.",
 s:{ev:["If there is a good, loving mind behind everything, then meaning, love and right and wrong fit naturally into reality. Defenders say nothing else fits as well."],
    ob:[{t:"Other worldviews also claim to explain these things.",r:"Defenders say they either stop short of objective grounding or smuggle in something like God. Critics disagree."}]},
 i:{ev:["<strong>Fit.</strong> A personal, good, purposive God makes objective meaning, dignity and obligation expected parts of reality rather than surprises.","<strong>Rivals.</strong> Defenders argue that impersonal moral Platonism or pantheism cannot explain why truth and goodness bind <em>persons</em>."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Atheist moral realism and Nagel-style views</strong> give other options without God.",r:"Defenders think these leave the match between mind and value as an unexplained coincidence. Critics say theism leaves its own mysteries."},{t:"<strong>This is the hardest step to prove</strong>, since it says no alternative could work.",r:"Agreed. It is a claim about every possible alternative, so it is difficult to support."}]},
 e:{ev:["The argument is an inference to the best explanation of the unity of mind, value and reality. Theism explains their congruence by a single personal source (Adams; Swinburne's argument from consciousness; Plantinga). Craig uses moral, aesthetic and consciousness arguments cumulatively."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Alternatives and parity.</strong> Nagel argues that consciousness and value point to a teleological natural order without theism. Wielenberg and Parfit defend non-theistic realism, and Platonic and Buddhist traditions offer other grounds. The claim that <em>only</em> theism can ground these features is a universal negative, and it requires comparing each worldview's total explanatory cost, including the problem of evil.",r:"Defenders argue the alternatives have ad hoc or mysterious elements. Critics say the same of theism, so the question becomes one of overall comparison."}],
    who:"Defend: Adams, Swinburne, Plantinga, Craig. Dispute: Nagel, Wielenberg, Parfit."}},

pb_pp1:{plain:"The source of these things must be a someone, not a something, because love and purpose belong to persons.",
 s:{ev:["Love, purpose and caring belong to people, not to rocks or forces. So whatever grounds them must be a person-like being."],
    ob:[{t:"Maybe they come from something impersonal that produces persons.",r:"Possible. Then the question is whether an impersonal source can really ground love and purpose."}]},
 i:{ev:["If love, purpose and will are objectively real and good, a personal source explains them directly, while an impersonal one would need to produce them from something quite different."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Effects can differ from causes.</strong> Minds came from non-minds in evolution.",r:"Defenders say that is about how minds arose, not about what grounds the <em>value</em> of love."}]},
 e:{ev:["A theistic ground for personal values (Swinburne; Adams) explains why moral and personal goods are of a piece, whereas an impersonal ground must explain how persons come to matter."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Emergence.</strong> Critics (Nagel; emergentists) argue that persons and their values can arise from a natural, even teleological order without a prior person.",r:"Defenders reply that 'emergence' labels rather than explains."}],
    who:"Defend: Swinburne, Adams. Dispute: Nagel."}},

pb_pp2:{plain:"The source must also be the standard of goodness itself.",
 s:{ev:["If good and bad are real, the thing behind them is itself perfectly good. It would be odd for the source of goodness to be less than good."],
    ob:[{t:"Why can't goodness just exist on its own?",r:"It might. That is the Platonic alternative, and this is the question of the moral argument."}]},
 i:{ev:["The ground of goodness is best seen as the perfect example of goodness, as in the theistic answer to the Euthyphro dilemma."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Goodness without God</strong> (Platonism, non-natural realism) is the rival picture.",r:"Defenders say it lacks an explanation of why goodness binds persons."}]},
 e:{ev:["Parallel to the moral argument: Adams identifies the Good with God, grounding goodness in a being who is its paradigm."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Same dispute as the moral argument.</strong> Wielenberg and Parfit deny that goodness needs a divine ground.",r:"See the moral argument premises."}],
    who:"Defend: Adams. Dispute: Wielenberg, Parfit."}},

pb_pp3:{plain:"The source must be beyond the physical universe, since it would have to exist whatever the universe is like.",
 s:{ev:["If meaning and goodness are real no matter how the universe turns out, their source cannot just be part of the universe."],
    ob:[{t:"They might depend on human nature, which is part of the universe.",r:"Then they would be less objective."}]},
 i:{ev:["Objective values would hold in any universe, so they need a ground that does not depend on the physical world: a necessary, transcendent reality."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Abstract necessary facts</strong> could play this role without being a being.",r:"Defenders say abstract facts cannot ground persons or purposes."}]},
 e:{ev:["Necessity and independence of contingent physical facts push the ground of objective value to a necessary reality (Adams's theistic Platonism; Plantinga on necessary being)."],
    ob:[{t:"<strong>Naturalistic and constructivist alternatives</strong> (Railton; Korsgaard) deny that objective value needs a transcendent ground.",r:"Defenders say these lose necessity or objectivity; critics say they keep enough of both."}],
    who:"Defend: Adams, Plantinga. Dispute: Railton, Korsgaard."}}
};
